WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:12.223
Picture a scene from the Indiana Jones adventure movie trilogy: The hero, a modern adventurer and archaeologist named Indiana Jones, is in the tropical jungles of India.

2
00:00:12.224 --> 00:00:20.357
He fearlessly climbs a tall cliff, travels through the rain forest, dodges poison arrows, and crosses a rope bridge.

3
00:00:20.358 --> 00:00:27.682
Despite their extreme exhaustion and the drizzling rain, Jones and his team keep walking through the mountains.

4
00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:34.839
Their destination is a ridge, reported to be the location of the ancient ruins of the "Temple of Doom."

5
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.114
The men stop to rest at a small clearing.

6
00:00:38.115 --> 00:00:42.428
Indiana Jones notices the rocks are unusually flat.

7
00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:48.250
He pulls away a vine to reveal a stone wall carved with words and pictures.

8
00:00:48.251 --> 00:00:55.759
All the men cheer, and Indiana cheers the loudest—at last they have found the Temple of Doom.

9
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.835
Is this story of Indiana Jones fact or fiction?

10
00:00:59.836 --> 00:01:03.335
"Fiction, of course!" would be the correct answer.

11
00:01:03.336 --> 00:01:13.332
But the inspiration for the daredevil explorer Indiana Jones was a real-life person, the American archaeologist and explorer Hiram Bingham.

12
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:20.649
Bingham had many adventures as he explored the jungles of South America in the early twentieth century.

13
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:31.980
However, Bingham’s greatest achievement was in 1911, when he made one of the most important archaeological discoveries of the century, Machu Picchu.

14
00:01:31.981 --> 00:01:37.558
Machu Picchu is a city in the Andes Mountains of Peru, South America.

15
00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:43.621
It was built by the Inca people between 1440 and 1470.

16
00:01:43.622 --> 00:01:52.294
It is sometimes called the Lost City of the Incas because it remained hidden in the jungle for over four hundred years.

17
00:01:52.295 --> 00:02:02.376
Machu Picchu is extraordinary because it is so well preserved and because it shows the exceptional skills and knowledge of the Incas.

18
00:02:02.377 --> 00:02:08.121
The Incas were a tribe who lived near the city of Cuzco, Peru, in the twelfth century.

19
00:02:08.122 --> 00:02:16.343
From the twelfth century to the mid-sixteenth century, the Inca Empire expanded and encompassed most of South America.

20
00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:23.424
The Incas were able to rule this territory because their society was very regimented and productive.

21
00:02:23.425 --> 00:02:34.092
The Incas developed highly sophisticated agriculture, architecture, engineering, government administration, education, health care, and fabric-making technology.

22
00:02:34.093 --> 00:02:39.567
Incan buildings were stable and able to withstand the region’s frequent earthquakes.

23
00:02:39.568 --> 00:02:47.321
Temples were constructed completely without mortar; instead the stones were cut to fit together exactly.

24
00:02:47.322 --> 00:02:53.923
Almost five hundred years later, Incan forts and temples stand in near-perfect condition.

25
00:02:53.924 --> 00:02:59.993
No other civilization in the world could cut and assemble stone blocks so perfectly.

26
00:02:59.994 --> 00:03:05.729
The biggest concern for the Incan government was feeding its massive empire.

27
00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:11.545
Growing crops was difficult in the high and rocky terrain of the Andes Mountains.

28
00:03:11.546 --> 00:03:21.381
However, the Incas designed farmland for corn, potatoes, and medicinal plants by shaping their hills into level strips of fertile ground.

29
00:03:21.382 --> 00:03:30.200
These terraces were so successful that in the fifteenth century, there was more land being cultivated in the area than there is today.

30
00:03:30.201 --> 00:03:39.782
Recently archaeologists set up sample crops using Incan farming methods and produced more food per acre than they could with modern methods.

31
00:03:39.783 --> 00:03:44.034
Incan government and education was also highly organized.

32
00:03:44.035 --> 00:03:50.356
All citizens in the Inca Empire aged 13 were required to take an intelligence test.

33
00:03:50.357 --> 00:03:59.263
If they failed the test, they were trained in a trade, and if they passed the test, they were sent to Cuzco and became public servants.

34
00:03:59.264 --> 00:04:07.501
It is believed that a few of these brilliant public servants were selected to work at Machu Picchu as assistants to the royal family.

35
00:04:07.502 --> 00:04:17.003
A position at Machu Picchu was special; it is estimated that a maximum of only 1,200 people resided there at any one time.

36
00:04:17.004 --> 00:04:27.252
The Incan royal family and their guests spent time there, but most residents were workers, including potters, stonemasons, silversmiths, goldsmiths, and weavers.

37
00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:39.828
Some experts believe that Machu Picchu was a summer retreat for Incan nobility and royalty because the Incan emperor at the time, Pachacuti, enjoyed its incredible natural beauty.

38
00:04:39.829 --> 00:04:48.132
But Machu Picchu was an attractive place for other reasons too; the weather was cool, and it was quiet and isolated.

39
00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:56.888
These factors made it an ideal place for recreation, reflection, and conducting business during the hot Peruvian summers.

40
00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:02.303
Other people think that the site was chosen because of its high altitude and isolation.

41
00:05:02.304 --> 00:05:06.803
It provided the perfect location for a secure military base.

42
00:05:06.804 --> 00:05:18.553
The royal family could use Machu Picchu to escape from any armed conflicts occurring below in Cuzco and also observe the Peruvian landscape for any potential threats.

43
00:05:18.554 --> 00:05:25.203
Machu Picchu consists of over two hundred stone buildings built in typically precise Incan fashion.

44
00:05:25.204 --> 00:05:32.703
In fact, the stone blocks were cut so exactly that it is impossible to slide even a credit card between them.

45
00:05:32.704 --> 00:05:40.172
The site features three distinct areas: the religious area, the military area, and the residential area.

46
00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:43.596
They are separated by a majestic square.

47
00:05:43.597 --> 00:05:51.833
The religious area has temples and a sundial known as the Intihuatana, or "sun-catcher," shaped out of natural rock.

48
00:05:51.834 --> 00:05:57.078
It was also used as an altar to worship the Incan sun god, Inti.

49
00:05:57.079 --> 00:06:02.482
A priest or the Incan king would give speeches next to the Intihuatana.

50
00:06:02.483 --> 00:06:09.170
The acoustics in the main square below are perfect, comparable to those of modern auditoriums.

51
00:06:09.171 --> 00:06:18.322
Machu Picchu was inhabited for about one hundred years, but its location was so remote that few people were aware of its existence.

52
00:06:18.323 --> 00:06:24.473
The Spanish conquered Peru in 1532, but they never found Machu Picchu.

53
00:06:24.474 --> 00:06:31.725
Over the next four hundred years, only a handful of farmers lived in this isolated and forgotten place.

54
00:06:31.726 --> 00:06:38.304
In 1911 Machu Picchu was reintroduced to the rest of the world by Hiram Bingham.

55
00:06:38.305 --> 00:06:45.214
Bingham and his team had set out from Lima, the capital of Peru, on an archaeological expedition.

56
00:06:45.215 --> 00:06:52.547
They were searching for Vilcabamba and Vitcos, two old Incan cities rumored to be hidden in the jungle.

57
00:06:52.548 --> 00:07:00.372
Bingham’s expedition traveled on foot and by mule through the Urubamba Valley and into the Urubamba Gorge.

58
00:07:00.373 --> 00:07:05.277
There he met local people who had heard about some old ruins nearby.

59
00:07:05.278 --> 00:07:10.601
They led Hiram Bingham to the ruins of Machu Picchu in November of that year.

60
00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:22.684
Initially Bingham was struck by the beauty and majesty of the city: "In the variety of its charms and the power of its spell," he wrote, "I know of no place in the world which can compare with it."

61
00:07:22.685 --> 00:07:29.351
Bingham made several trips back to Machu Picchu and took over eleven thousand photos of the site.

62
00:07:29.352 --> 00:07:40.672
In 1913 the site received a significant amount of publicity after National Geographic magazine devoted its entire April 1913 issue to it.

63
00:07:40.673 --> 00:07:50.413
Later Bingham wrote a number of books and articles about Machu Picchu; his book Lost City of the Incas became a best seller.

64
00:07:50.414 --> 00:07:57.230
Today Machu Picchu is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a popular tourist attraction.

65
00:07:57.231 --> 00:08:02.308
In 2003 four hundred thousand people visited Machu Picchu.

66
00:08:02.309 --> 00:08:07.149
Despite its remote location, it is easy for a tourist to visit.

67
00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.160
First, fly to Peru’s capital, Lima.

68
00:08:10.161 --> 00:08:16.564
Then, from Lima, take another short flight to the city of Cuzco in the southern part of the country.

69
00:08:16.565 --> 00:08:22.790
Once in Cuzco, you can hire a guide and hike to Machu Picchu along the Incan Trail.

70
00:08:22.791 --> 00:08:25.536
This takes about four days on foot.

71
00:08:25.537 --> 00:08:36.297
Alternatively you can take a short train ride to Aguas Calientes, the nearest town to Machu Picchu, and then take a twenty-minute bus ride up to the ruins.

72
00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:44.026
Either way you will arrive at the breathtaking Machu Picchu, a sight like nothing else you will ever experience!

